[![Crates.io](https://img.shields.io/crates/v/graphql-rs)](https://crates.io/crates/graphql-rs)
[![Documentation](https://img.shields.io/badge/docs-docs.rs-blue)](https://docs.rs/graphql-rs)
[![License: MIT OR Apache-2.0](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT%20OR%20Apache--2.0-blue)](LICENSE)
[![Build Status](https://img.shields.io/github/actions/workflow/status/SuperInstance/graphql-rs/ci.yml)](https://github.com/SuperInstance/graphql-rs/actions)
[![Coverage](https://img.shields.io/codecov/c/github/SuperInstance/graphql-rs)](https://codecov.io/gh/SuperInstance/graphql-rs)

> **10x Simpler GraphQL for Rust** - Type-safe, high-performance GraphQL server with zero boilerplate

## Why graphql-rs?

Building GraphQL APIs in Rust shouldn't be complicated. graphql-rs makes it **simple**:

- **10x Less Code** - Define schemas in minutes, not hours
- **Type-Safe** - Full Rust type safety without complex macros
- **Lightning Fast** - Zero-allocation parsing, efficient batching
- **Production-Ready** - Built-in caching, DataLoader, subscriptions
- **Framework Agnostic** - Works with Actix, Rocket, Warp, Axum
- **Developer Friendly** - Clear error messages, great documentation

### The 10-Second Hook

```rust
use graphql_rs::{Schema, resolver};

resolver! {
    Query {
        fn hello() -> String {
            "Hello, GraphQL!".to_string()
        }

        fn user(id: ID) -> User {
            db::get_user(id)
        }
    }
}

let schema = Schema::builder().query(Query).build()?;
let response = schema.execute("{ hello }").await?;
```

That's it. No complex macros. No boilerplate. Just Rust.

## Key Features

- **🚀 Simple API** - Define schemas and resolvers in natural Rust
- **⚡ High Performance** - Zero-allocation parsing, efficient DataLoader batching
- **🔌 Subscriptions** - First-class WebSocket support with state machine reliability
- **🏗️ Federation** - Native Apollo Federation support for microservices
- **💾 Built-in Caching** - Response caching, field caching, custom backends
- **📊 DataLoader** - Automatic batching to solve N+1 problems
- **🔒 Type-Safe** - Full type safety without proc-macro complexity
- **🛠️ CLI Tools** - Development server, code generation, validation
- **📚 Great Docs** - Comprehensive guides and examples
- **🔌 Integrations** - Works with docstore-rs, vectordb-rs, metrics-rs, and more

## Quick Start

### Installation

```toml
[dependencies]
graphql-rs = "0.1"
tokio = { version = "1.0", features = ["full"] }
```

### Your First GraphQL API

```rust
use graphql_rs::{Schema, Resolver};

// Define your types
#[derive(GraphQLType)]
pub struct User {
    pub id: String,
    pub name: String,
    pub email: String,
}

// Write resolvers
pub struct Query;

impl Resolver for Query {
    fn hello(&self) -> String {
        "Hello, GraphQL!".to_string()
    }

    fn user(&self, id: String) -> Option<User> {
        db::get_user(&id)
    }

    fn users() -> Vec<User> {
        db::all_users()
    }
}

pub struct Mutation;

impl Resolver for Mutation {
    fn create_user(&self, input: CreateUserInput) -> User {
        db::create_user(input)
    }
}

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() -> graphql_rs::Result<()> {
    // Build schema
    let schema = Schema::builder()
        .query(Query)
        .mutation(Mutation)
        .build()?;

    // Execute query
    let query = r#"
        {
            users {
                id
                name
                email
            }
        }
    "#;

    let response = schema.execute(query).await?;
    println!("{}", serde_json::to_string_pretty(&response)?);

    Ok(())
}
```

### Run a Development Server

```bash
# Install CLI
cargo install graphql-rs-cli

# Start dev server with hot-reload
graphql serve

# Visit http://localhost:4000/playground
```

