### graphql-rs vs Juniper

**Juniper:**
```rust
// Complex macros, lots of boilerplate
#[derive(GraphQLObject)]
struct User {
    id: i32,
    name: String,
}

struct Context;
impl juniper::Context for Context {}

struct Query;
#[graphql_object(context = Context)]
impl Query {
    fn user(ctx: &Context, id: i32) -> FieldResult<User> {
        // fetch user
    }
}
```

**graphql-rs:**
```rust
// Simple, clear, type-safe
#[derive(GraphQLType)]
struct User {
    id: String,
    name: String,
}

resolver! {
    Query {
        fn user(id: ID) -> User {
            db::get_user(id)
        }
    }
}
```

**~5x less code, same functionality**

## Common Use Cases

### 1. API Gateway

Build a unified GraphQL API over multiple microservices:

```rust
let gateway = Gateway::builder()
    .service("users", "http://users-service/graphql")
    .service("posts", "http://posts-service/graphql")
    .build()?;
```

### 2. Real-Time Subscriptions

WebSocket subscriptions with automatic reconnection:

```rust
subscription! {
    Subscription {
        userCreated -> User {
            pubsub.subscribe("user_created")
        }
    }
}
```

### 3. DataLoader Batching

Automatically solve N+1 query problems:

```rust
resolver! {
    User {
        #[batched]
        fn posts(&self) -> Vec<Post> {
            // Automatically batched with other User.posts calls
        }
    }
}
```

### 4. Caching

Built-in response and field caching:

```rust
resolver! {
    Query {
        #[cache(ttl = 60)]
        fn user(id: ID) -> User {
            db::get_user(id)
        }
    }
}
```

